The sixth impossibility theorem of Arrhenius demonstrates the mutual inconsistency of the following five conditions: Egalitarian Dominance, General Non-Extreme Priority, Non-Elitism, Weak Non-Sadism and Weak Quality Addition.  

Critical-level utilitarianism (and its close variants) evaluates a population by the sum of the individual distances from a fixed positive critical level \(c>0\). Because the underlying rule remains a finite, continuous, impartial sum, the view satisfies the four “positive” conditions:  
- same-size egalitarian improvements raise the sum (Egalitarian Dominance);  
- no lexical or extreme priority is given to the worst-off (General Non-Extreme Priority);  
- sufficiently many modest gains can always outweigh a single large gain (Non-Elitism);  
- sufficiently many lives well above \(c\) can always outweigh any fixed population of negative lives (Weak Quality Addition).  

The same rule, however, violates Weak Non-Sadism. Lives whose welfare lies in the open interval \((0,c)\) receive a negative score and therefore detract from the total; lives with modestly negative welfare can receive a less negative score. Consequently there exist situations in which the addition of negative-welfare lives is ranked above the addition of positive-welfare lives—precisely the content of Weak Non-Sadism.  

Hence critical-level views escape the impossibility result only by failing Weak Non-Sadism.  

