The provided code has a critical flaw in data handling: `generate_noisy_data` emits all class-0 points followed by all class-1 points, and the subsequent split therefore yields a training set that is 60 % class 0 while the test set consists solely of the left-hand tail of class 1.  Consequently every reported test accuracy is simply the fraction of those out-of-distribution points that the network happens to label 1.

A two-layer ReLU network of width 32 is sufficiently expressive to fit a curved decision surface on the support of the training data.  Outside that support the surface is unconstrained by the loss; the inductive bias of gradient descent on a ReLU MLP extends the class-0 region into the whole left-hand half-plane, producing the observed ~16 % test accuracy.

Reducing every hidden width from 32 to 2 (option A) collapses the hypothesis class to functions that are only mildly non-linear.  In particular the architecture can still realise a pure linear classifier (one hidden unit realises \(\operatorname{ReLU}(y)\), the other realises \(\operatorname{ReLU}(-y)\), and the remaining layers recombine them into a signed multiple of \(y\)).  The optimal linear separator for the training distribution is essentially a horizontal threshold on the \(y\)-coordinate.  All of the held-out test points lie well below that threshold and are therefore labelled 1, raising test accuracy to approximately 85 %.  No other listed change forces the same simplicity bias:

- larger width (I) restores the ability to form a curved surface and the same harmful extrapolation;
- learning-rate or epoch-count adjustments (B–D,H) either leave the network under-fitted (~50 %) or let it reach the same bad minimum;
- batch-size changes (E,G) do not alter the hypothesis class;
- the illegal `train_size=10000` (F) produces an empty test set and a runtime exception.

Hence only the width reduction systematically maximises the printed average test accuracy.

